WEBVTT

00:09.117 --> 00:11.627
Gran, what's that bright star?

00:14.525 --> 00:18.875
That's not a star, love. That's Saturn.

00:19.125 --> 00:20.655
How can you tell?

00:21.008 --> 00:25.818
See how it shines so steady?
Stars twinkle. Planets don't.

00:27.108 --> 00:30.778
A real planet? Can we go there?

00:31.117 --> 00:36.697
Let's imagine we can. But
let's go the long way round.

00:36.847 --> 00:39.817
We'll start in the middle, with the Sun.

00:39.857 --> 00:41.857
But the Sun's gone.

00:42.500 --> 00:48.840
It hasn't gone anywhere. The Earth spins,
and right now our side is facing away from it.

00:48.990 --> 00:51.280
So it's round the other side!

00:52.817 --> 00:54.107
Have a look.

00:57.300 --> 00:59.110
It's got rings!

00:59.260 --> 01:03.560
Later. Sun first. Hold on tight.

01:06.308 --> 01:08.168
We're flying!

01:12.500 --> 01:17.530
This is the Sun. It's a
star: our very own star.

01:17.580 --> 01:19.430
It's so bright!

01:19.480 --> 01:22.400
It's about four and a
half billion years old.

01:22.450 --> 01:24.340
Older than Gran!

01:24.390 --> 01:25.600
Only just.

01:27.750 --> 01:29.180
Where's Earth?

01:29.430 --> 01:31.360
That little dot.

01:31.910 --> 01:36.370
The Sun is about a hundred
times wider than Earth.

01:37.108 --> 01:40.818
It would take more than a
million Earths to fill it up.

01:40.868 --> 01:44.258
That's a lot of Earths.

01:45.717 --> 01:53.357
Its surface is about five and a half
thousand degrees Celsius. And its middle is fifteen million.

01:53.507 --> 01:56.417
My bath is only forty!

01:57.858 --> 02:04.658
Almost all the stuff in our solar system
is in the Sun: ninety-nine point eight percent.

02:05.008 --> 02:08.568
The planets are just the crumbs.

02:09.933 --> 02:15.693
The real Earth is much farther away:
more than a hundred Suns in a row.

02:15.943 --> 02:22.413
Its light reaches Earth in about eight
minutes. And Earth goes once round the Sun every year.

02:22.663 --> 02:25.903
I've been round eight times. I'm eight!

02:26.253 --> 02:31.713
And remember, love: never look at the
Sun through a telescope. Never ever.

02:31.963 --> 02:34.683
Never ever. I promise.

02:36.833 --> 02:43.373
Mercury: the smallest planet, only a
little bigger than our Moon. And the closest to the Sun.

02:43.623 --> 02:46.603
It's so fast!

02:47.053 --> 02:52.563
The fastest of all. It whizzes round
the Sun in just eighty-eight days.

02:55.708 --> 02:58.668
So how old would I be on Mercury?

02:58.918 --> 03:03.668
You're eight in Earth years. In
Mercury years, you'd be thirty-three.

03:04.118 --> 03:07.988
Thirty-three! I'm a grown-up!

03:08.238 --> 03:10.328
You'd still go to bed at eight.

03:12.892 --> 03:23.242
With almost no air to hold in the heat,
its days reach four hundred and thirty degrees, and its nights drop to minus one hundred and eighty.

03:23.792 --> 03:27.482
I'd need my scarf and my sun hat.

03:31.225 --> 03:36.345
Ah, Venus. When I was little, I
called her the evening star.

03:36.395 --> 03:39.195
But she's not a star, Gran!

03:39.345 --> 03:41.465
Quite right.

03:41.815 --> 03:46.415
After the Moon, Venus is the
brightest thing in our night sky.

03:49.375 --> 03:53.595
It's almost the same size as
Earth. Some call it Earth's twin.

03:54.545 --> 03:55.775
Could we live there?

03:59.508 --> 04:06.838
Not a chance. Under those thick clouds,
it's the hottest planet of all. Hot enough to melt lead.

04:06.988 --> 04:08.818
Hotter than Mercury?

04:08.868 --> 04:18.098
Hotter than Mercury, even though Mercury
is closer to the Sun. The longest a robot probe lasted down there was just over two hours.

04:18.348 --> 04:21.098
Oh, poor robots.

04:23.667 --> 04:29.267
And Venus spins backwards. There,
the Sun rises in the west.

04:29.517 --> 04:37.067
And Venus spins so slowly that one turn
takes longer than its whole year!

04:37.217 --> 04:39.957
So I'd get a birthday every day!

04:40.407 --> 04:41.767
Nice try.

04:45.725 --> 04:46.815
There's us!

04:47.265 --> 04:51.775
Earth: the third planet
from the Sun. Our home.

04:53.317 --> 05:00.067
It's the only place we know of with living
things. And about seventy-one percent of its surface is ocean.

05:00.217 --> 05:03.717
And that's our side, in the dark!

05:04.858 --> 05:09.048
Hello, Gran! I can see you from up here!

05:09.498 --> 05:11.128
I'm right behind you, love.

05:11.278 --> 05:17.968
You know, every other planet is named
after a god. Earth just means 'the ground'.

05:19.508 --> 05:21.078
Is the Moon far?

05:21.228 --> 05:25.758
About thirty Earths could fit in
a row between us and the Moon.

05:32.892 --> 05:34.632
Has anyone been there?

05:34.782 --> 05:41.502
Twelve people have walked on it. And in
2026, four astronauts flew all the way around it.

05:42.267 --> 05:46.927
I watched the first moonwalk
on a tiny television.

05:47.077 --> 05:50.117
I'm going there. I'll jump really high.

05:52.275 --> 05:57.185
You would. On the Moon, you'd
weigh only a sixth as much.

05:57.435 --> 06:02.785
And every year, the Moon drifts about
four centimetres farther away.

06:03.235 --> 06:04.815
Come back, Moon!

06:08.675 --> 06:10.555
Are we nearly at Saturn?

06:10.655 --> 06:14.615
Not even close. This is Mars.

06:14.865 --> 06:20.245
The red planet. It's red because
its dusty ground is full of rust.

06:22.192 --> 06:24.112
Could I live on Mars?

06:24.362 --> 06:35.792
It's about half as wide as Earth. A day
there is only a little longer than ours. But the air is thin, and mostly carbon dioxide. You couldn't breathe it.

06:38.642 --> 06:46.242
It has the biggest volcano in the solar
system: Olympus Mons. It's almost three times as tall as Mount Everest.

06:46.992 --> 06:48.672
Are there any Martians?

06:48.822 --> 06:54.642
Only robots. Mars is the only planet
we know of where everyone is a robot.

06:57.208 --> 07:02.868
A year on Mars is 687
days. Nearly two of ours.

07:03.218 --> 07:06.328
So on Mars, I'd be four?

07:06.428 --> 07:07.858
Just over four.

07:08.308 --> 07:12.708
Four again. Heaven help us.

07:15.875 --> 07:18.195
Rocks! Hold on, Gran!

07:26.692 --> 07:28.132
Don't worry.

07:28.282 --> 07:31.212
Oh. I was holding my breath.

07:31.562 --> 07:39.802
This is the asteroid belt, between Mars
and Jupiter. There are millions of rocks here, left over from when the solar system was made.

07:45.558 --> 07:54.908
But space is so big that they're very far
apart. The big ones are, on average, nearly eight times farther apart than Earth and the Moon.

07:56.258 --> 08:02.318
Put them all together, and they'd still
weigh less than our Moon. Spacecraft travel through quite safely.

08:08.875 --> 08:10.535
Is that Saturn?

08:10.685 --> 08:19.525
Not yet. This is Jupiter, the biggest
planet of all. If it were hollow, about a thousand Earths could fit inside.

08:23.083 --> 08:25.973
What's that red spot? An eye?

08:26.123 --> 08:32.593
A storm. A giant storm, bigger than Earth,
that has been raging for hundreds of years.

08:34.525 --> 08:41.285
And it spins faster than any other planet.
A day on Jupiter lasts only about ten hours.

08:41.435 --> 08:43.375
That's how long I sleep!

08:46.042 --> 08:55.342
It has more than a hundred moons. The four
biggest were first seen in 1610, by Galileo, with one of the very first telescopes.

08:55.692 --> 08:59.342
And with your telescope, you can
see those four from our hill.

08:59.492 --> 09:01.062
Tomorrow night!

09:01.612 --> 09:08.232
One of them, Ganymede, is the biggest moon
of all. It's even bigger than the planet Mercury.

09:11.800 --> 09:16.700
One trip round the Sun takes
Jupiter about twelve of our years.

09:16.950 --> 09:22.430
So your first Jupiter birthday
is when you're nearly twelve.

09:22.580 --> 09:25.030
Twelve years for one cake?

09:25.280 --> 09:28.050
One very big cake.

09:35.408 --> 09:37.958
Saturn! At last!

09:38.108 --> 09:39.918
It's got rings!

09:40.168 --> 09:42.618
And now it's later.

09:44.767 --> 09:46.577
Is it far from the Sun?

09:46.727 --> 09:49.997
So far that sunlight takes about
eighty minutes to get here.

09:51.850 --> 09:53.180
Is it big?

09:53.230 --> 09:55.590
Nine times wider than Earth.

09:58.133 --> 10:00.113
Could I stand on it?

10:00.263 --> 10:05.693
No. Saturn has no solid ground.
It's mostly hydrogen and helium.

10:06.143 --> 10:10.853
It's so light for its size,
it would float in a bath.

10:11.003 --> 10:14.733
We'd need a really big bath.

10:17.067 --> 10:19.337
What are the rings made of?

10:19.487 --> 10:26.877
Billions of chunks of ice and rock: some
as small as dust, some as big as a house.

10:30.808 --> 10:34.788
And the main rings are only
about ten metres thick.

10:36.933 --> 10:38.363
Does it have a moon?

10:40.113 --> 10:42.773
Hundreds! More than any other planet.

10:45.342 --> 10:49.662
And one year on Saturn lasts
about twenty-nine Earth years.

10:49.912 --> 10:53.402
So on Saturn, I'm not even one!

10:53.552 --> 10:55.292
How far does it go, Gran?

10:55.442 --> 10:59.532
Let's find out. Hold on tight.

11:03.500 --> 11:09.810
In 1781, a man called William Herschel was
looking through his telescope, just like you.

11:09.960 --> 11:14.780
He spotted a little dot, and
thought it was a comet, or a star.

11:15.030 --> 11:17.530
Just like me and Saturn!

11:17.880 --> 11:23.440
It was a planet: Uranus. The first
planet ever found with a telescope.

11:23.690 --> 11:28.630
He wanted to name it after
the King. King George.

11:28.680 --> 11:31.770
Planet George!

11:33.700 --> 11:36.030
Why is it so blue?

11:36.280 --> 11:45.250
A gas called methane. And look: Uranus is
tipped right over. It rolls around the Sun on its side, like a ball.

11:48.800 --> 11:53.970
So parts of it have a winter
twenty-one years long, in the dark.

11:54.120 --> 11:56.860
Twenty-one years of winter?

11:56.910 --> 11:59.070
Better pack a bigger scarf.

12:08.250 --> 12:12.200
Look, Gran. The Sun's
just a little star now.

12:12.750 --> 12:18.780
Neptune: the farthest planet. Sunlight
takes four hours to get here.

12:21.950 --> 12:27.680
It's the windiest planet. Its winds blow
faster than two thousand kilometres an hour.

12:32.250 --> 12:38.350
You can't see Neptune with just your
eyes. People found it with sums first.

12:38.900 --> 12:47.750
One year here is a hundred and sixty-five
of ours. Since it was discovered, Neptune has been round the Sun only once.

12:48.100 --> 12:50.250
Not even Gran is that old.

12:50.500 --> 12:53.020
Thank you, love.

13:01.450 --> 13:05.420
Look! That one's got a heart!

13:05.670 --> 13:09.840
That's Pluto: a dwarf planet,
smaller than our Moon.

13:10.190 --> 13:15.250
And it was named by a girl of eleven,
at breakfast with her grandad.

13:15.400 --> 13:17.570
A girl named a planet?

13:17.720 --> 13:19.420
She did.

13:21.158 --> 13:28.948
Pluto lives in the Kuiper Belt: a huge
ring of icy worlds out past Neptune. There may be millions of them.

13:34.042 --> 13:38.602
On average, sunlight takes about five
and a half hours to reach Pluto.

13:39.352 --> 13:47.132
And the spacecraft Voyager 1 is out even
farther. So far that its messages take a whole day to get home.

13:51.167 --> 13:56.967
And our Sun is just one of more than a
hundred billion stars in our galaxy.

13:57.717 --> 13:59.037
Where's home?

14:05.167 --> 14:14.627
In 1990, from far past Neptune, Voyager 1
looked back one last time and took a picture of home.

14:15.377 --> 14:18.137
Earth was a single point of light.

14:19.387 --> 14:20.527
That's us?

14:20.877 --> 14:25.887
That's us. Everyone we've ever
loved is on that little dot.

14:32.079 --> 14:35.279
Gran, we're on the dot. Right now.

14:35.329 --> 14:38.419
Right now, love. Both of us.

14:50.158 --> 14:53.948
Gran, that's not a star. That's Venus.

14:54.558 --> 14:57.328
How can you tell?

14:57.368 --> 15:01.608
It shines so steady. Stars
twinkle. Planets don't.

15:01.648 --> 15:03.578
That's my clever girl.

15:06.433 --> 15:09.293
We turned back round to the Sun!

15:09.443 --> 15:12.493
And that light left it eight minutes ago.

15:14.400 --> 15:16.650
Thank you for the trip, Gran.

15:16.700 --> 15:19.190
Thank you for the questions.

15:19.540 --> 15:21.270
Same time tomorrow night?

15:21.320 --> 15:24.670
Same time tomorrow night.
